            <ArticleTitle>McKittrick-Wheelock syndrome: a rare case report of acute renal failure.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>301-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.15386/cjmed-536</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Giant tubular-villous adenoma of the rectum can determine secretory diarrhea, associated with a depleting syndrome of prerenal acute renal failure, hyponatremia, hypokalemia and hypoproteinemia. These symptoms are known as the McKittrick-Wheelock syndrome, and there are about 50 cases reported in literature. We present the case of a 59-year-old woman presented to our emergency department with abdominal pain, prerenal azotemia, and electrolyte disturbances with a background of chronic diarrhea, caused by a giant rectal tumor. Conservative therapy initially improved and normalized renal function, and made surgical resection of the tumor possible.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
